<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Itō Hirobumi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a>–<a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ito Hirobumi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Itō Hirobumi (1841–1909) was a Japanese statesman from the Chōshū domain and the first prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, serving four times between 1885 and 1901. He led the drafting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889 and founded the Rikken Seiyūkai party in 1900. As the first Resident-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from 1905 he oversaw the Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and he was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> in 1909 by the Korean nationalist An Jung-geun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi.jpg" alt="Itō Hirobumi" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IT%C5%8C_Hirobumi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Itō Hirobumi was born Hayashi Risuke on 16 October 1841 in Tsukari village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suo-province">Suō Province</a>, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Chōshū Domain</a>, the son of a farmer of humble origins. His father entered the service of a low-ranking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, Itō Naoemon, and was adopted with his household into the Itō family, which gave them samurai status at the lowest rank. His name became Itō Shunsuke in 1858 and Hirobumi around 1869. In 1863 he was one of the Chōshū Five who secretly travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s ban on overseas travel, and studied at University College London.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868 his knowledge of the West advanced him. He became the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyogo-prefecture">Hyōgo Prefecture</a>, and in 1870 and 1871 he toured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to study finance, which influenced the New Currency Regulation of 1871 that put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the gold standard. From late 1871 to 1873 he was one of four deputy ambassadors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwakura-mission">Iwakura Mission</a> to the United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where his ability to speak English made him useful. On his return he sided with those who opposed a military expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and was appointed councilor and minister of public works.</p><p>In the Political Crisis of 1881 he used Ōkuma Shigenobu's proposal for a British-style parliament to force Ōkuma's resignation and secured agreement on a gradual, Prussian-influenced course, emerging as first among equals in the Meiji oligarchy. He then travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and from 1884 headed the bureau drafting the constitution. In 1885 he replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daijo-kan">Daijō-kan</a> with a cabinet and became the first prime minister. He led the Privy Council that reviewed the constitution before its promulgation in 1889.</p><p>His pragmatism earned him the nickname Round Itō. He accepted reluctantly that a government party was needed, an idea Yamagata and the other elder statesmen vetoed in 1892, and he founded the Rikken Seiyūkai in 1900.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Itō oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> and negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>'s surrender on terms that included the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan-korea-treaty-of-1905">Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905</a> he became the first Resident-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. He first supported a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> rather than annexation, but pressure from the army leadership and the failure of his gradualist approach contributed to his support for annexation. He resigned in June 1909 and four months later was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> by the independence activist An Jung-geun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Itō Hirobumi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The genro, an informal council of elder statesmen such as <b>Ito Hirobumi</b> and Yamagata Aritomo, provided coherent direction through the Meiji and Taisho periods, but as this generation died the system produced no institutional successor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Kido Takayoshi and Takasugi Shinsaku, pupils of Yoshida Shōin, led the loyalists of Chōshū, whose Kiheitai militia, under Yamagata Aritomo, helped seize the domain government; <b>Itō Hirobumi</b>, Inoue Kaoru and Ōmura Masujirō joined them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Its men, Kido Takayoshi, <b>Ito Hirobumi</b> and Yamagata Aritomo, led Meiji Japan; it became Yamaguchi Prefecture in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>The Eulsa Treaty of 1905 made Korea a Japanese protectorate under Resident-General <b>Ito Hirobumi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/korean-empire">Korean Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tsukari, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Harbin railway station, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.01,132.01&amp;year=1885" aria-label="Tsukari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7913 931 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Itō Hirobumi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8363 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8363 1201h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.01,132.01&amp;year=1885">What was here in 1885? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
